  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 PM CST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nUpper ridging continues to influence the central CONUS this \nafternoon, while the poignant upper trough rotates through the \nPacific Northwest bringing areas of heavy rain and snow. As the \nsurface ridge axis shifts east into the Tennessee Valley this \nevening, southerly return flow begins to amplify late tonight as the \nincoming western trough deepens, tightening the pressure gradient by \nsunrise Friday. Short range guidance exhibits good agreement on \nsouth-southwest winds increasing from 15 to 25 mph sustained, \ngusting to 45 mph throughout the afternoon. The persistent warm air \nadvection and additional boundary layer mixing will keep or warm \nhighs by a few degrees to near 70 degrees. Increasing low-level Gulf \nmoisture transport ahead of the cold front should mitigate \nwidespread fire concerns. However, it may be worth monitoring grass \nfire ignition, especially towards north central Kansas where wind \ngusts are highest and fuels are becoming dormant.  \n\nOn Saturday, the upper trough ejects from the Rockies into the \nCentral Plains, driving a cold front southeastward during the \nafternoon and evening hours. Ensemble solutions show decent \nagreement on widespread showers developing along and behind the \nfront, highest being towards central and eastern Kansas(70-100%). \nCluster analysis from ensembles still has some discrepancies on \ntiming and moisture availability with the potential for a secondary \nfrontal wave late Saturday evening, providing higher QPF amounts to \nat least 1 inch or higher by Sunday morning. Majority of the latest \nensemble guidance, however are leaning towards a more progressive \nwave and therefore lower QPF amounts. Lapse rates on forecast \nsoundings are not ideal for severe weather with elevated instability \nbeing <500 J/KG. Effective shear though is decent near 50 kts and \nperpendicular to the boundary so cannot rule out the possibility for \nan isolated sustained updraft to produce large hail or gusty winds. \n\nEarly next week, models are converging on a cold Canadian surface \nhigh building southward across the Central Plains.  Persistent CAA \nand light northeast winds cap daytime highs to the lower and middle \n40s each afternoon, while mostly cloudy skies and good radiational \ncooling carry overnight lows into the low 20s. Although a weak, \nsouthern stream disturbance passes to the south Tuesday and \nWednesday, ensembles are favoring dry conditions due to the meager \ndeep-layer moisture.",
